From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joshndroid <fmxjoshiewah@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 22:30:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] manager: keep a WoWLAN Wi-Fi connection that survived suspend

Devices with Wake-on-LAN or WoWLAN are skipped when the system goes to
sleep and taken down after it wakes, so that their connectivity is
checked again. For Wi-Fi with WoWLAN this throws away an association
the firmware kept through the whole suspend: on every resume the device
goes unavailable, disconnected, scans and reconnects. On a phone, which
suspends many times an hour, each wake costs about 13 seconds without
network and drops every open connection, and a suspend attempted during
the reconnect can fail while the driver holds a wakeup source after the
new connection.

When a Wi-Fi device with WoWLAN is still activated and its link still
has carrier after the wake, renew its dynamic IP configuration instead,
the way software devices are handled. If the association was lost
while asleep the link has no carrier, and the device is taken down as
before.

Tested on a Google Pixel 8 Pro (bcmdhd, s2idle) with NetworkManager
1.56.1: before, every wake left Wi-Fi unavailable for about 13 seconds;
with this change the device stayed activated through 8 suspends of 20 s
to 5 minutes, including suspends 5 s after a wake, with no lost pings
to the gateway and the DHCP lease renewed on each wake.
---
 src/core/nm-manager.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/core/nm-manager.c b/src/core/nm-manager.c
index 87dde2c..78e50cd 100644
--- a/src/core/nm-manager.c
+++ b/src/core/nm-manager.c
@@ -7400,6 +7400,23 @@ do_sleep_wake(NMManager *self, gboolean sleeping_changed)
                 if (nm_device_is_software(device))
                     continue;
 
+                /* A Wi-Fi device whose WoWLAN kept it associated through suspend is
+                 * still up: renew its DHCP lease instead of tearing the connection
+                 * down. Phones suspend many times an hour and every teardown costs
+                 * a scan, a full reconnect and the loss of open connections.
+                 */
+                if (device_is_wake_on_lan(priv->platform, device)
+                    && nm_device_get_device_type(device) == NM_DEVICE_TYPE_WIFI
+                    && nm_device_get_state(device) == NM_DEVICE_STATE_ACTIVATED
+                    && nm_platform_link_is_connected(priv->platform,
+                                                     nm_device_get_ip_ifindex(device))) {
+                    _LOGD(LOGD_SUSPEND,
+                          "sleep: device %s still connected after wake, renewing",
+                          nm_device_get_ip_iface(device));
+                    nm_device_update_dynamic_ip_setup(device, "wake up");
+                    continue;
+                }
+
                 /* Belatedly take down Wake-on-LAN devices; ideally we wouldn't have to do this
                  * but for now it's the only way to make sure we re-check their connectivity.
                  */
